= The Succession Trials: 1964 and 1966

When Jawaharlal Nehru passed away on 27 May 1964, international observers openly questioned whether Indian democracy could outlive its founding prime minister. Western commentators predicted military takeover or political balkanisation; yet India pulled off two successive, peaceful leadership transitions in less than two years.

- *The First Succession — Lal Bahadur Shastri (May 1964):*
  - *The consensus choice:* K. Kamaraj, then Congress President, consulted party MPs and chief ministers. The party unanimously chose *Lal Bahadur Shastri* — a senior minister renowned for integrity, modesty, and administrative competence.
  - *A smooth democratic miracle:* The transition proved to the world that India's constitutional institutions did not depend on one individual.
  - *Shastri's dual crises:*
    - *Economic crisis:* Severe monsoon failures, widespread food grain scarcity, and dependence on American PL-480 wheat imports.
    - *The 1965 Indo-Pak War:* Pakistani military infiltration in Kashmir triggered full-scale armed conflict across the western border.
  - *The immortal rallying cry:* Shastri coined the slogan *"Jai Jawan, Jai Kisan"* (Hail the Soldier, Hail the Farmer), linking the soldier fighting at the frontier with the farmer producing food in the fields.
  - *The Tashkent tragedy (10 January 1966):* Shastri signed the UN/Soviet-brokered *Tashkent Agreement* with Pakistan's President Ayub Khan (mediated by Soviet Premier Alexei Kosygin). Hours after signing, Shastri died suddenly of a heart attack in Tashkent, throwing India into another succession test.

- *The Second Succession — Indira Gandhi versus Morarji Desai (January 1966):*
  - *An open contest:* Unlike 1964, consensus failed. *Morarji Desai* (former Chief Minister of Bombay and senior minister) insisted on a democratic vote against *Indira Gandhi* (daughter of Nehru and then Minister of Information and Broadcasting).
  - *The secret ballot:* Congress MPs voted by secret ballot. Indira Gandhi defeated Morarji Desai decisively, winning *355 votes to 169* (over two-thirds support).
  - *Why the Syndicate backed Indira:* The powerful regional bosses (the "Syndicate") calculated that Indira was politically inexperienced and would remain dependent on their guidance.
  - *Lohia's mocking label:* Socialist leader Rammanohar Lohia famously ridiculed Indira Gandhi as *"Gungi Gudiya"* (the Silent Doll) due to her hesitation in parliamentary debates — a label she would shatter within three years.

#analogy-box(title: "The Regents and the Young Queen")[
  A powerful council of court elders (the Syndicate) installs a young, untested princess (Indira Gandhi) on the throne, believing she will serve as their decorative figurehead while they run the kingdom from behind the curtain. Instead, the young ruler builds a direct, devoted relationship with the common people outside the palace gates, fires the court elders one by one, and converts the royal council into a personal command staff. The mapping: the elders = the Syndicate bosses; the princess = Indira Gandhi; the direct popular pact = the "Garibi Hatao" coalition.
]

= The Fourth General Elections (1967): The "Political Earthquake"

The February 1967 General Elections were the first national elections held without Jawaharlal Nehru. The country was simmering with discontent, and the election results shattered the unshakeable hegemony Congress had enjoyed since 1952.

- *The Crisis Atmosphere of 1967:*
  - *Economic breakdown:* Two successive drought years, acute agricultural collapse, industrial recession, and double-digit inflation.
  - *The Rupee devaluation:* In June 1966, under heavy pressure from the US and the World Bank, Indira Gandhi devalued the Indian Rupee from ₹5 to over ₹7 per US dollar. The decision stoked widespread public outrage and price escalation.
  - *Popular protests:* Widespread bandhs, strikes, student unrest, and violent food riots broke out across the country. The government responded with police firing, worsening alienation.

- *Rammanohar Lohia and "Non-Congressism":*
  - *The core thesis:* Socialist thinker *Dr. Rammanohar Lohia* argued that Congress rule was undemocratic, elitist, and opposed to the interests of the impoverished masses.
  - *The electoral mathematics:* Congress stayed in power not because it had majority popular support, but because the non-Congress majority vote was split among dozens of rival opposition parties.
  - *The strategy:* Lohia advocated *Non-Congressism* — urging all non-Congress parties (regardless of ideology, from the Hindu-nationalist Jan Sangh to the Left) to form united electoral fronts to defeat the Congress monolith and protect Indian democracy.

- *The Verdict — The "Political Earthquake":*
  - *Lok Sabha decline:* Congress retained power at the Centre with its lowest seat tally (283 out of 520) and lowest popular vote share (40.8%) since 1952. Half of Indira Gandhi's cabinet ministers were defeated.
  - *Defeat of the Syndicate giants:* Syndicate kingpins lost their own seats — K. Kamaraj in Madras, S.K. Patil in Bombay, Atulya Ghosh in West Bengal.
  - *Loss of State Assemblies:* Congress lost power in *eight states* (seven failed to win majorities; an eighth, Haryana, was lost to post-poll defections).
  - *The Non-Congress geographical belt:* An unbroken stretch of non-Congress governments spanned northern and eastern India: Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal, Orissa, Madras (Tamil Nadu), and Kerala.
  - *The DMK historic triumph:* In Madras State, the *Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam (DMK)* swept to power on an anti-Hindi platform, becoming the first regional party in independent India to secure a clear single-party majority in a state legislative assembly.

= Coalitions and Defections: The SVD Phenomenon

The 1967 elections inaugurated two defining features of contemporary Indian politics: multi-party coalition governments and opportunistic legislative defections.

- *Samyukta Vidhayak Dal (SVD) Governments:*
  - *Strange bedfellows:* In states where no single party had a majority, joint legislative parties called *Samyukta Vidhayak Dal* were cobbled together.
  - *Ideological contradictions:* In Uttar Pradesh and Bihar, the SVD ministries brought the right-wing *Bharatiya Jana Sangh*, the centrist *Swatantra Party*, the socialist *SSP/PSP*, and the communist *CPI* into the same cabinet.
  - *Inherent fragility:* Held together solely by anti-Congress opportunism rather than shared programs, SVD governments suffered continuous internal bickering, frequent cabinet collapses, and recurring spells of President's Rule.

- *The Defection Epidemic — "Aaya Ram, Gaya Ram":*
  - *Definition:* A defection occurs when an elected legislator leaves the political party under whose symbol they won election and shifts allegiance to another party or coalition.
  - *The origin of the phrase:* In Haryana (1967), an independent MLA named *Gaya Lal* changed his party allegiance three times in a single fortnight — moving from Congress to United Front, back to Congress, and then back to United Front within nine hours!
  - *The press conference:* Haryana United Front leader Rao Birender Singh brought Gaya Lal to Chandigarh journalists and declared: *"Gaya Ram is now Aaya Ram."*
  - *The institutional fallout:* Defections became the primary instrument to topple ministries and install puppet chief ministers in Haryana, Madhya Pradesh, and Uttar Pradesh. The scourge persisted until the 52nd Constitutional Amendment enacted the Anti-Defection Law in 1985.

