= The Split in the Congress (1969)

The defeat of the Syndicate in 1967 gave Indira Gandhi the opening to assert her authority. The simmering factional battle culminated in the open rupture of the ruling party in 1969.

- *The Underlying Divide — The Syndicate vs. Indira:*
  - *Who was the Syndicate?* A powerful informal clique of conservative party bosses: *K. Kamaraj* (Tamil Nadu, ex-Congress President), *S. Nijalingappa* (Mysore, Congress President), *S.K. Patil* (Bombay), *Atulya Ghosh* (West Bengal), and *N. Sanjeeva Reddy* (Andhra Pradesh).
  - *The core friction:* The Syndicate demanded that the Prime Minister consult the party organisation on all major policy and appointment decisions. Indira insisted on the absolute executive supremacy of the Prime Minister.

- *Indira's Radical Leftward Pivot:*
  - *The Ten-Point Programme (May 1967):* Social control of banking institutions, nationalisation of general insurance, ceiling on urban property, state trading in foodgrains, public distribution systems, and land reforms.
  - *The July 1969 Economic Package:*
    - *Bank Nationalisation:* Indira bypassed Parliament and issued an emergency ordinance nationalising *14 major private commercial banks*.
    - *Abolition of Privy Purses:* Announced the termination of hereditary cash pensions and privileges guaranteed to 565 former princely rulers under Article 291 and 362 of the Constitution.
    - *Morarji's exit:* When Finance Minister Morarji Desai expressed reservations, Indira unilaterally stripped him of the Finance portfolio. Desai resigned in protest.

- *The Catalyst — The 1969 Presidential Election:*
  - *The vacancy:* President Dr. Zakir Husain passed away in May 1969.
  - *The Syndicate's nominee:* The Congress Parliamentary Board, dominated by the Syndicate, nominated *N. Sanjeeva Reddy* (Speaker of the Lok Sabha) as the official Congress candidate, ignoring Indira's objections.
  - *Indira's counter-move:* Indira secretly encouraged Vice-President *V.V. Giri* to resign and contest as an Independent candidate.
  - *The whip vs. the conscience vote:*
    - Congress President S. Nijalingappa issued an official party whip directing all Congress MPs and MLAs to vote for Sanjeeva Reddy.
    - Nijalingappa held talks with opposition parties (Jan Sangh, Swatantra), giving Indira the moral high ground to accuse the Syndicate of colluding with right-wing reactionaries.
    - On the eve of voting, Indira Gandhi openly called upon electors to cast a *"free conscience vote"* (vote according to inner judgment, defying party whips).
  - *The election result:* V.V. Giri won the presidency in a dramatic contest, defeating the official Congress candidate Sanjeeva Reddy.

- *The Formal Rupture (November 1969):*
  - On 12 November 1969, Congress President Nijalingappa formally expelled Prime Minister Indira Gandhi from the Indian National Congress for indiscipline.
  - The party fractured into two rival organisations:
    - *Congress (O) — Organisation:* Led by the Syndicate and Morarji Desai (the "Old Congress").
    - *Congress (R) — Requisitionists:* Led by Indira Gandhi and Jagjivan Ram (the "New Congress").
  - *Survival as a minority government:* Indira lost her majority in the Lok Sabha, dropping to 220 seats. However, her government survived comfortably with issue-based external support from the *Communist Party of India (CPI)* and the *DMK*.

